  <section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><div class="breadcrumb">Home / <a href="/journal/">Journal</a> / Tax & relocation</div><span class="eyebrow">Tax & relocation · 25 October 2026 · 6 min read</span><h1 style="font-size:clamp(40px,6vw,74px);margin-top:20px;max-width:1000px">Italy’s €300,000 flat tax: what international residents should know</h1><p class="lead">Italy’s lump-sum tax regime can be relevant to certain internationally mobile individuals considering a move to Italy. The headline figure is only the starting point.</p></div></section>
  <div class="page-hero-image"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1454165804606-c3d57bc86b40?auto=format&fit=crop&w=2200&q=88" alt="Documents, notebook and calculator on a desk" /></div>
  <article class="container article-body">
    <p>Italy has a special optional tax regime for certain individuals who transfer their tax residence to the country. Often described as the “flat tax” or “lump-sum tax” regime for new residents, it allows eligible taxpayers to pay a fixed annual substitute tax on qualifying foreign-source income, instead of applying ordinary Italian income tax rules to that foreign-source income covered by the option.</p>
    <p>It is designed for a specific profile of taxpayer and requires careful assessment. It is not a general tax discount available to every foreign buyer, nor does it automatically cover Italian-source income.</p>
    <h2>What changed for 2026?</h2>
    <p>Under the 2026 Budget Law, the annual substitute tax is <strong>€300,000 for the principal taxpayer</strong> for individuals transferring their residence to Italy from 1 January 2026. The regime can also be extended to eligible family members for an additional <strong>€50,000 per family member per year</strong>, subject to the applicable rules.</p>
    <p>Different amounts may continue to apply to taxpayers who transferred residence in earlier years under the transitional rules: broadly, €100,000 for those who entered the regime before the August 2024 increase and €200,000 for those within the subsequent period before 2026. The precise treatment should be confirmed by a tax professional based on the date of transfer and the taxpayer’s situation.</p>
    <h2>Who may qualify?</h2>
    <p>Among other conditions, the regime generally requires that the individual has not been tax resident in Italy for at least nine of the ten tax periods preceding the start of the option. Eligibility and the scope of foreign-source income must be checked against the relevant law and the facts of each case.</p>
    <p>The annual lump sum is not a percentage of income: it is a fixed amount, regardless of the amount of qualifying foreign-source income covered by the regime. That feature may make the regime relevant to some high-net-worth individuals, but it does not make it advantageous in every case. Income-source rules, assets, family circumstances, treaty questions and other tax regimes can materially affect the analysis.</p>
    <h2>What does the regime not mean?</h2>
    <ul><li>It does not mean that all income worldwide is automatically exempt from ordinary Italian tax.</li><li>It does not remove the need to establish tax residence correctly or to meet the conditions for the option.</li><li>It does not replace property-specific checks, legal advice, inheritance planning or advice on income and assets outside the regime.</li><li>It should not be assumed to combine with every other tax incentive.</li></ul>
    <h2>Why this matters when considering Lake Como</h2>
    <p>For an international buyer, a property decision may be one part of a broader relocation or lifestyle plan. Timing, tax residence, ownership structure, the intended use of the property and the role of family members can all deserve attention before making commitments. A property adviser can help coordinate the project and introduce relevant specialists, while tax and legal conclusions should be provided by qualified professionals who have reviewed the full circumstances.</p>
    <h2>A sensible next step</h2>
    <p>Before relying on the headline figure, ask a qualified Italian tax adviser to assess eligibility, the relevant date of transfer, the source of each income stream, the duration and cost of the option, and the interaction with any other applicable regime. A preliminary discussion can help identify the right questions, but it is not a substitute for individual advice.</p>
